# Core + Security

## Agent behavior

- Match existing patterns; reuse shared components before inventing new ones.
- Do not expand scope, add unrequested features, or create docs/markdown unless asked.
- Prefer editing existing files over adding new ones; extract only when reused 2+ times.
- Ask before large refactors, new dependencies, or destructive git operations.
- Never commit secrets (`.env`, keys, tokens). Use env vars only.

## Stack (do not replace)

- Backend: Node.js, Express, PostgreSQL, Sequelize, JWT, CommonJS JS
- Frontend: React, TypeScript, Vite, Tailwind, react-i18next, axios, lucide-react
- Auth: JWT in `localStorage` (`token`); `ProtectedRoute` + role guards

## Security

- Protect every private route with `authenticate` + `authorize(role)` on the backend.
- Validate/sanitize on the server; never trust client-only checks (including entitlements).
- Parameterized Sequelize queries only — no string-built SQL.
- Do not log passwords, tokens, or full PII payloads.
- CORS stays locked to `FRONTEND_URL`; uploads stay under controlled `/uploads`.
- Frontend API 401 → clear token and redirect to `/login` (existing `api.ts` behavior).
- Do not extend “remember me” password storage patterns; do not store credentials in plaintext/base64.

```js
// ❌ BAD — unprotected or client-trusted auth
router.get('/items', controller.list);

// ✅ GOOD
router.get('/items', authenticate, authorize('procuring_entity'), controller.list);
```

```ts
// ❌ BAD
localStorage.setItem('password', value);

// ✅ GOOD — store only JWT; secrets in env
Authorization: `Bearer ${token}`
```
